<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Reading the default example</h2>
    <p>The calculator opens with a $395 card and $19,000 of yearly spending: $7,000 on travel and dining at 3 points per dollar and $12,000 elsewhere at 1 point. That earns 33,000 points, worth $412.50 at 1.25¢ each. Add the credits you'd actually use ($480 of the $580 advertised), subtract the fee, and the card nets $497.50 a year. A no-fee 2% card would return $380 on the same spending, so the premium card comes out $117.50 ahead.</p>
    <p>That lead is thinner than it looks, because it rests on 2 assumptions.</p>

    <h2>Assumption 1: what a point is worth</h2>
    <p>The 2 cards tie at a point value of 0.89¢. Redeem at 1¢ as plain cash back and the premium card's lead shrinks to $35; anything below 0.89¢ and the free card wins. Transfer partners can push points well past 1.25¢, but only if you actually book those redemptions. Enter the value you got on your last few redemptions, not the best case from a travel blog.</p>

    <h2>Assumption 2: which credits you'll really use</h2>
    <p>The default counts the $300 travel credit in full, 75% of the lounge visits, and half the streaming credit. If you'd use only the travel credit, credits drop to $300 and the card nets $317.50, which is $62.50 behind the no-fee card. A credit is worth its face value only when it replaces money you would have spent anyway. A $10 monthly credit at a service you don't otherwise use is worth $0 to you, however it's marketed.</p>
    <p>A useful test: look at your last 12 statements and total the credits that actually posted. That number belongs in the calculator, not the advertised total.</p>

    <h2>Keep, downgrade, or cancel</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Downgrade</strong> to a no-fee card from the same issuer when one exists. The account stays open, so your credit history and limit are preserved.</li>
      <li><strong>Canceling</strong> removes that card's limit from your available credit, which raises your utilization. With $5,000 of balances across $25,000 of limits, utilization is 20%; close a card with a $10,000 limit and it becomes 33%, which can lower your credit score.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask before you decide.</strong> Issuers sometimes offer a retention bonus or fee credit when you call to cancel or downgrade. Some also refund a fee if you close the account soon after it posts; check your card's terms for the window.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A sign-up bonus changes only the first year. The "Years you'll keep the card" field spreads it over your holding period so a one-time bonus doesn't make a card look permanently better than it is.</p>
  </div>
</section>
